/**
@mainpage Simple Crypts C API

[Home and demos](../index.html) · [Fleet](../examples/fleet_manager/index.html)

The C99 core uses caller-owned bounded storage and synchronous provider callbacks.
Applications own transport: producing a frame never delivers it. Each context represents
one role and one peer. Serialize access externally; callbacks must not reenter that context.

Start with @ref initialization and @ref host. Embedded integrations implement @ref providers.
Use @ref enrollment before @ref credits or @ref resources; exchange complete frames through
@ref transport. @ref fixtures contains test helpers, not application provisioning APIs.

@par Working example
The following Linux example creates a temporary server, authorizes enrollment and generates
an invitation. A real application retains its private store directory and securely provisions
its enrollment secret. The example does not transmit or approve anything automatically.
@include api_example.c

@anchor provider_contract
@par Provider contract
Callback inputs are borrowed for the synchronous call; never retain stack or output buffers.
The provider's user state and schema descriptors must outlive the context. Report success
only after outputs are complete. Do not publish partial results from failed callbacks.

Commit compares the durable generation, atomically replaces the encoded record and advances
the generation. Generation zero denotes absence. Reserve persists the nonce high-water mark
before success. An uncertain storage outcome must fail closed until normal reload reconciles
it; never roll back reservations. Reinitialize with sc_init(), never a copied sc_context or
restored runtime nonce cursor. Private keys remain inside the provider.

@par Enrollment and time
Signed enrollment requires explicit server authorization and approval of the exact
serial/session/key binding. The application supplies trusted server time in consistent units
and chooses the expiry interval. sc_receive() has no trusted time and fails closed for
unapproved signed server sessions; use sc_receive_at() instead.

@par Credits and reporting
Credits issued and consumed are cumulative uint64 totals. Consumption changes the device's
local state. The server sees the last reported consumption until a request, response and
receipt exchange completes. Neither setters nor requests perform transport I/O.

@par Further reading
[Protocol](https://github.com/PseudoDesign/simple-crypts/blob/main/docs/protocol.md),
[Platform contracts](https://github.com/PseudoDesign/simple-crypts/blob/main/docs/platforms.md),
[Resource encoding](https://github.com/PseudoDesign/simple-crypts/blob/main/docs/resources.md),
and [Quality checks](https://github.com/PseudoDesign/simple-crypts/blob/main/docs/quality.md).
*/
